  "type": "singleCorrect",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "If $x y+y^{2}=\\tan x+y,$ then find $\\frac{d y}{d x}$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "W14D9",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\sec ^{2} x}{x+2 y}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "2VhI3",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\sec ^{2} x-y}{(x+2 y-1)}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "UoC7Y",
      "text": "$(x+2 y-1) \\sec ^{2} x$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ig2mu",
      "text": "$\\sec ^{2} x \\cdot y$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The given relation is $x y+y^{2}=\\tan x+y$. Differentiating both sides with respect to $x$, we get\r\n\r\n$\\frac{d}{d x}(x y)+\\frac{d}{d x}\\left(y^{2}\\right)=\\frac{d}{d x}(\\tan x)+\\frac{d y}{d x}$\r\n\r\nor $\\left[y \\cdot 1+x \\cdot \\frac{d y}{d x}\\right]+2 y \\frac{d y}{d x}=\\sec ^{2} x+\\frac{d y}{d x}$\r\n\r\nor $(x+2 y-1) \\frac{d y}{d x}=\\sec ^{2} x-y$\r\n\r\n$\\therefore \\frac{d y}{d x}=\\frac{\\sec ^{2} x-y}{(x+2 y-1)}$",
    "image": null
  },
